n10.text:
You claw at the plaster with your bare hands and it comes away in slabs, and your nails split, and behind it, packed like bricks, are the babies. Twelve, thirteen, you lose count, small grey husks in rotted cloth that roll out into your arms and spill across your feet, light as dried fruit and wet like old mushrooms where they split. Something small bursts under your heel.

{{SQUELCH}}

The stench hits like a fist and you go down on your knees and vomit between them, hot and sour, onto their faces. All of them are ruined. All except one, right at the front, whose face is smooth and pink, like it is only sleeping. Its mouth is open a little. Do you reach inside?

n12.text:
The kitchen knife is on the floor, which you don't remember bringing. You put the tip at the notch of the throat and lean, and the skin doesn't cut, it holds, tight as a drumhead.

{{POP!}}

Then it gives, a wet crack like a grape, and you drag the blade down. Ribs thin as bird bones bend under the steel and snap.

{{CRACK. CRACK. CRACK.}}

Something dark slides out over your wrist, unbelievably hot, and you hear yourself moan, a low animal noise. In the grease of the open chest lies a rectangle of yellow paper. You grab it with your bleeding hand and the baby's face turns toward you and from under the closed lids two thin red tears run down into its ears, and the mouth opens, and the whole house fills with one high clear note.

{{hmmmmm...}}

Like a child humming.

n13.text:
You get both thumbs under the little chin and PULL. The jaw resists, then the joint goes.

{{KRACK!}}

Wet and loud, and the whole lower half of the face hangs down on a strip of grey skin, and the sound that comes out of the mouth is not a cry, it's the stale air of a hundred years leaving a body. The paper is right there on the tongue. You pluck it out with two shaking fingers, and it's warm and soaked and writing-side up, and the little eyes open. Both of them. They look at you, wet and black and without any surprise, and a single red drop rolls out of each and down the cheek, and the open mouth says, in a voice like wind in a pipe, 'Thank you.'
